\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\paragraph{Methodological pitfalls.}
\label{sec:pitfalls}
Lead-lag analysis in iterative model collapse is fragile along three axes, each documented with data from \S\ref{sec:regime}--\ref{sec:dose_response}.
\emph{Method sensitivity}: five detection methods applied to identical data yield forward significance rates spanning 0\% to 92\%, with inter-method agreement at $\kappa{=}0.185$.
\emph{Spurious regression}: first-differencing eliminates canary$\to$perplexity associations ($F{\approx}0$, $p{=}0.99$) while preserving canary$\to$diversity (24/24$\to$24/24), exposing an entire analysis channel as trend-driven~\cite{granger1974spurious}.
\emph{Regime dependence}: the same Granger test shifts from 0/8 to 6/8 significant pairs within a 2-percentage-point interval ($\alpha{=}0.48 \to 0.50$).

\subsection{Method Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:mmct}
We compare five lead-lag detection methods---naive cross-correlation, differenced cross-correlation, threshold onset, permutation Granger causality (PermG), and Toda--Yamamoto~\cite{toda1995statistical}---in a multi-method comparison~\cite{simonsohn2020specification} calibrated against null-control ($\alpha{=}0.0$) data where any detection is a false positive.
Detection results use equal-weight majority voting ($K{\geq}3$ of 5 methods, each contributing one vote per pair) with forward-direction filtering; FPR-inverse weighting is used only in exploratory method ranking (Appendix~\ref{app:weight_robustness}).
Each method embodies different assumptions: naive cross-correlation is assumption-free but produces 92\% null-control FPR; differenced cross-correlation controls for trends but lacks directional separation; threshold onset is power-limited at $T{=}11$; Toda--Yamamoto avoids pre-testing for unit roots; PermG uses a nonparametric permutation null with built-in directionality.
Pooled null-control FPR across three seeds (cluster-bootstrap 95\% CI): PermG 0\% $[0, 0]$, Toda--Yamamoto 17\% $[0, 25]$, DiffXCorr 38\% $[25, 50]$, NaiveXCorr 92\% $[88, 100]$ (Appendix~\ref{app:fpr_independence}).
DiffXCorr's empirical FPR (38\%) substantially exceeds its simulated rate (4.2\% under AR(1) DGP), indicating that model-collapse nonstationarity extends beyond simple autoregressive structure.
Under all four weighting schemes (equal, FPR-inverse, capped, rank-based), PermG is the only method maintaining zero null-control FPR alongside consistent detection at $\alpha \geq 0.50$.
This finding---that method choice alone can shift conclusions from universal significance to complete null on identical data---motivates reporting method sensitivity as a primary result rather than relegating it to a robustness check.

\paragraph{Permutation Granger causality.}
PermG tests whether past values of canary metric $X$ improve prediction of downstream metric $Y$ beyond $Y$'s own history; Granger precedence establishes temporal predictive improvement, not true causality~\cite{shojaie2022granger}.
We apply 5{,}000 permutations~\citep{phipson2010permutation} on first-differenced series with Benjamini--Hochberg FDR correction at $q{=}0.05$ across 8 validated canary$\to$downstream pairs (2 canaries $\times$ 4 downstream metrics).
The permutation test controls Type~I error exactly by construction (exchangeable null distribution); BH-FDR correction remains valid under positive regression dependence~\cite{benjamini2001control}, and our 0/24 null-control rejections across three seeds validate this theoretical guarantee (within-seed ICC${=}0.34$, cluster-adjusted 95\% CI $[0\%, 41\%]$; Appendix~\ref{app:fpr_independence}).
Simulation under non-stationary AR(1) DGP ($\varphi{=}0.95$--$0.99$) reveals PermG FPR inflation to ${\sim}10\%$; however, our empirical series exhibit low stochastic persistence (null token entropy $\varphi{=}-0.51$; first-differenced distinct-1 $\varphi{<}0.44$), and 0/24 null rejections confirm adequate size control (Appendix~\ref{app:nonstationarity}).
The same simulation shows PermG retains ${\sim}80\%$ of raw-levels power at $T{=}11$ versus ${\sim}61\%$ for standard first-differencing, justifying the permutation approach at short series lengths.

\subsection{Detection Boundary}
\label{sec:theory}

We model the PermG detection rate as a $T$-dependent step function, excluding the non-monotonic dip at $\alpha \in [0.55, 0.65]$:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{rate}(\alpha, T) = \frac{0.70}{1 + \exp\!\bigl(-100\,(\alpha - \alpha_c(T))\bigr)},
\label{eq:joint-sigmoid}
\end{equation}
fitted jointly to $T{=}10$ ($n{=}17$) and $T{=}20$ ($n{=}5$) data ($R^2{=}0.883$).
The detection boundary follows a power law in observation length:
\begin{equation}
\alpha_c(T) = 0.193 + 75.68 \cdot T^{-2.41}.
\label{eq:alpha-c}
\end{equation}
The empirical exponent $\gamma{=}2.41$ admits a parametric explanation under a thresholded sub-linear signal model.

\begin{proposition}[Detection phase boundary]\label{prop:phase-boundary}
Under a sub-linear signal model $S(\alpha) = c \cdot \max(0,\, \alpha - \alpha_\infty)^\beta$ with $\beta < 1$, the detection boundary satisfies
\begin{equation}
\alpha_c(T) = \alpha_\infty + A \cdot T^{-1/(2\beta)},
\label{eq:alpha-c-theory}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_\infty$ is the detection floor, $A = (z^* \sigma / c)^{1/\beta}$, and $z^* = \Phi^{-1}(\pi^*)$ \textup{(proof in Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_c_proof})}.
\end{proposition}

Fitting to Eq.~\ref{eq:alpha-c} yields $\alpha_\infty{=}0.193$, $\beta{=}0.208$, and $\gamma_{\mathrm{theory}} {=} 1/(2\beta) {=} 2.408$, recovering the empirical $\gamma{=}2.41$ ($\Delta\gamma{=}0.002$; joint model $R^2{=}0.883$ vs.\ $0.711$ for linear $\beta{=}1$; Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_c_proof}).
The floor $\alpha_\infty{=}0.193$ implies contamination below ${\sim}19\%$ produces no causal signal regardless of $T$; at $T{=}5$, $\alpha_c \geq 1.0$.
The steepness $k{=}100$ yields a step-like transition ($\alpha_c(10) {=} 0.487$, $\alpha_c(20) {=} 0.248$; \S\ref{sec:regime}, \S\ref{sec:t20}).
Signal $S(\alpha)$ rises monotonically, yielding four regimes: \emph{blind} ($S$ below floor), \emph{onset} ($S$ crosses threshold), \emph{non-monotonic dip} ($\alpha{\approx}0.60$; transient, dissolves by $T{=}25$), and \emph{detection} ($S$ dominates).

\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\phantomsection\label{app:alpha_c_proof}
\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:phase-boundary}.}
At the detection threshold, $\mathrm{Power}(\alpha_c, T) = \pi^*$.
Since $\alpha_c$ lies below the non-monotonic dip region ($\alpha_c < 0.55$), the signal model applies directly.
From the power function $\mathrm{Power} = \Phi(S(\alpha) \cdot \sqrt{T} / \sigma)$, setting $S(\alpha_c) \cdot \sqrt{T} / \sigma = z^*$ and substituting the signal model $S(\alpha) = c(\alpha - \alpha_\infty)^\beta$ yields $c(\alpha_c - \alpha_\infty)^\beta = z^* \sigma / \sqrt{T}$.
Solving: $\alpha_c(T) = \alpha_\infty + (z^* \sigma / c)^{1/\beta} \cdot T^{-1/(2\beta)}$, giving the stated form with exponent $\gamma = 1/(2\beta)$.

\paragraph{Calibration and consistency.}
Fitting the theory model to 7 extrapolated points from Eq.~\ref{eq:alpha-c} ($T \in \{8, 10, 12, 15, 20, 25, 30\}$) yields $\alpha_\infty = 0.193$, $\beta = 0.208$, $A = 75.38$, $\gamma_{\mathrm{theory}} = 1/(2\beta) = 2.408$ (near-perfect by construction, since both models share the same functional form; the meaningful comparison is the joint sigmoid fit to raw data: $R^2 = 0.883$ vs.\ $0.711$ for a linear signal model).
The empirical power-law fit (Eq.~\ref{eq:alpha-c}) gives $\gamma = 2.41$, a discrepancy of $\Delta\gamma = 0.002$.
A linear signal model ($\beta = 1$, implying $\gamma = 0.5$) achieves $R^2 = 0.711$ and is rejected.
The sub-linear exponent $\beta = 0.208$ implies that doubling contamination above floor increases signal by only $2^{0.208} \approx 1.15\times$ (15\%), explaining the wide blind zone at short $T$.
At $T = 5$, the model predicts $\alpha_c = 0.193 + 75.38 \cdot 5^{-2.408} \geq 1.0$: no contamination level is detectable, consistent with the insufficient statistical power at very short observation windows.

\section{T=20 Predict-then-Verify Comparison}
\label{app:t20_comparison}

Table~\ref{tab:t20_comparison} reports the predict-then-verify results for the $T{=}20$ extension experiments.
Predictions are derived from the detection boundary model (\S\ref{sec:theory}): detected pairs at $T{=}10$ are predicted to remain detected (saturated $F$-statistics), and undetected pairs are predicted to remain undetected (structural nulls or non-monotonic dip).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{T=20 predict-then-verify comparison (Pythia-410M, seed~42, WikiText, fp32). Excluding $\alpha{=}1.0$, two of four predictions fall within CI ($\alpha{=}0.50$, $0.75$); boundary cases show systematic bias---$\alpha{=}0.45$ falsifies the ceiling hypothesis (under-prediction), $\alpha{=}0.60$ over-predicts under raw $p$-values (v1; matches under FDR v2, not pre-committed).}
\label{tab:t20_comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
$\alpha$ & $T{=}10$ & Predicted $T{=}20$ & Observed $T{=}20$ & Match \\
\midrule
0.45 & 0/8 & 0/8 (ceiling) & 5/8 & $\times$ \\
0.50 & 6/8 & 6/8 (saturated) & 6/8 & \checkmark \\
0.60 & 0/8 & 3/8 (v1 raw)$^\dagger$ & 0/8 & $\times$ \\
0.75 & 6/8 & 6/8 (saturated) & 6/8 & \checkmark \\
1.00 & 6/8 & 6/8 (saturated) & 6/8 & \checkmark \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$V2 (BH--FDR corrected, created 44\,min pre-analysis, not git-committed) predicts 0/8, matching observation.} \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At $\alpha \geq 0.50$, consistency across $T{=}10$ and $T{=}20$ supports the detection boundary prediction: PermG counts are determined by signal structure, not observation length.
Six diversity-based pairs show $F{=}449$--$2947$ at $T{=}20$ (all $\gg F_{\text{crit}}$); two MAUVE-based pairs show $F{<}2$ regardless of $T$, driven by floor saturation ($\mu{=}0.029$, 100\% ${<}0.05$) rather than high variance (CV$_{\text{MAUVE}}{=}0.089$ vs CV$_{\text{distinct-1}}{=}0.139$); SNR: MAUVE $4.68$ vs diversity $6.40$--$8.20$ ($p{<}0.001$).
The sole exception is $\alpha{=}0.45$ at $T{=}20$ (5/8), consistent with boundary proximity.
At $\alpha{=}0.45$, the shift from 0/8 to 5/8 falsifies Model~A (ceiling: $\alpha{=}0.45$ structurally undetectable) in favor of Model~B (power-limited: $\sqrt{T}$ boost crosses the detection floor), lowering $\alpha_c$ below~0.45.
The $\alpha{=}0.60$ non-monotonic dip persists at $T{=}20$ (s42: 0/8; s43: 3/8) but dissolves at $T{=}25$ (5/8), confirming that $\sqrt{T}$ power scaling eventually overcomes the dip; alternative methods detect signal throughout (DiffXCorr 6/8, TY 5/8 at $T{=}20$).
PermG directional selectivity is consistent across tested $T$ ($T{=}10$, $20$): forward 6/8 versus reverse 0/8 at $\alpha \in \{0.50, 0.75, 1.0\}$.


\section{T=20 Prediction Timeline}
\label{app:t20_prediction_timeline}

Two versions of the $T{=}20$ prediction file exist, reflecting an evolving statistical protocol:

\paragraph{V1 (git-committed, \texttt{bad72cc}).} Predictions derived from $T{=}10$ PermG results using \emph{raw} $p$-values. At $\alpha{=}0.60$, v1 counted 3/8 pairs with raw $p{<}0.05$, predicting 3/8 detections at $T{=}20$. The observed result (0/8 FDR-significant) does not match.

\paragraph{V2 (pre-analysis, not committed).} A revised file incorporating BH--FDR correction at $q{=}0.05$, consistent with the correction applied throughout the paper. Under FDR, $\alpha{=}0.60$ drops from 3/8 (raw) to 0/8, matching the observation. V2 was created 44 minutes before the formal $T{=}20$ analysis began (filesystem timestamp) but was not git-committed, and therefore lacks the cryptographic verifiability of v1.

\paragraph{Scoring.} Primary (v1, committed): 3/5 exact matches ($\alpha \in \{0.50, 0.75, 1.0\}$, all saturated regimes). Excluding $\alpha{=}1.0$, two of four predictions fall within CI. Both boundary predictions miss: $\alpha{=}0.45$ (predicted 0/8, observed 5/8) falsifies the ceiling hypothesis in favor of $\sqrt{T}$ power scaling; $\alpha{=}0.60$ (predicted 3/8, observed 0/8) over-estimates detection due to the non-monotonic dip. Sensitivity (v2, FDR-corrected): 4/5 ($\alpha{=}0.60$ matches). The systematic conservative bias at $\alpha{=}0.45$ is consistent with the $\sqrt{T}$ power increase predicted by Proposition~\ref{prop:phase-boundary}.


